    "result_text": "All 287 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Requirement:** Reject creating an owner when the owner's city already contains 8 owners, responding with 400.\n\n**Implementation** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`): After resolving the canonical city name, the code already counts existing owners in that city (`ownersInCity`, case-insensitive). I added a guard that returns `400 Bad Request` when that count is 8 or more:\n\n```java\nif (ownersInCity >= 8) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);\n}\n```\n\n**Tests** (`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests`):\n- Added `createOwnerRejectedWhenCityFull` \u2014 8 owners in a city \u21d2 9th (supplied with different casing) is rejected 400.\n- Added `createOwnerAllowedWhenCityHasRoom` \u2014 7 owners \u21d2 8th succeeds (201), confirming the boundary.\n\n**Reconciliation of a conflict with existing tests:** The two daily-signup-cap tests seeded 19/20 owners *all in \"Madison\"* and one (`createOwnerAllowedWhenBelowDailyLimit`) expected the next Madison create to succeed \u2014 which the new 8-per-city cap correctly blocks. Since those tests exist to exercise the *daily* boundary, not the city, I spread their seeded owners across unique cities (and used a fresh city for the created owner). This preserves each test's original intent while removing the incidental collision with the new per-city limit.",
